      "message": "To make sure I\u0027m following everything correctly in viz/chrome\u0027s side of things, these BackendTextures are being created and managed by WrappedGraphiteTextureBacking, which is the class used for a SharedImage that is entirely read/written to from within Skia/Graphite (WrappedSkImageBacking being the Ganesh one, and *not* a generic backing implemented in terms of SkImage).\n\nThe WrappedGraphiteTextureBacking creates the BackendTexture objects using `Recorder::createBackendTexture()` and then calls `deleteBackendTexture()` when the WrappedGraphiteTextureBacking is destroyed. Because `deleteBackendTexture()` actually calls `Destroy()` on the underlying wgpu::Texture, the WrappedGraphiteTextureBacking can\u0027t be deleted until after the command buffer has been submitted (or finished?).  The way this is achieved right now is by snapping Recordings, inserting, and submitting the Recordings aggressively.\n\nBackendTexture is an awkward vehicle for all of this because it\u0027s entirely out of the loop for all of the lifetime tracking of a Texture within Skia. Adding an extra SkRefCnt that is shared between a BackendTexture and every Texture that wraps it feels like a brittle solution. Is there a reason that WrappedGraphiteTextureBacking has to be implemented in terms of BackendTexture? What if instead of storing BackendTexture, you store the SkImage or SkSurface. Internally these are already holding refs on the DawnTexture resource that holds the wgpu::Texture. When the WrappedGraphiteTextureBacking is deleted and nothing else has usage refs on the DawnTexture, it will destroy its GPU data once the command buffer ref drops to 0 (and since the BindGroup cache is in terms of wgpu::Texture and not DawnTexture, that is not contributing to refs that keep the Texture alive from Skia\u0027s POV).\n\nIn terms of operations on the WrappedGraphiteTextureBacking:\n - ReadbackToMemory is already implemented by adopting the BackendTexture into an SkImage and calling asyncRescaleAndReadPixels on the SkImage. This would be trivial to move to the direct SkImage ownership model.\n - Initialize() appears to create a new BackendTexture and wgpu::Texture, but with no initial data. There\u0027s no direct SkImages factory for such an operation, but you can just as easily take this BackendTexture and immediately wrap it in an SkImage. While clients are still responsible for managing the lifetime of BackendTextures, this can be handled by using the release callback to destroy the backend texture when it\u0027s called (called once when the SkImage\u0027s Texture resource is destroyed, which should be after any CommandBuffer is finished).\n - InitializeWithData() could be handled similarly to Initialize(), but maybe it would be worth adding SkImage factories that could create a texture-backed image from an SkPixmap or compressed data directly (instead of first creating a raster-backed SkImage and calling TextureFromImage, or going through this createBackendTexture + updateBackendTexture multistep process).\n - For scoped read accesses, the vector of graphite backend textures is instead a vector of graphite-backed SkImages. If it\u0027s a single plane SharedImage then it just returns that one directly; if it\u0027s a multiplanar format, then it can call SkImages::TextureFromYUVImages (taking SkImages instead of BackendTextures for each plane).\n - For scoped write accesses, the initialization should create an SkSurface first and remember that, and can always get its SkImage view if a read access is ever required.\n - UploadFromMemory would require some modifications on the part of the Recorder/Context API to accept an SkImage/SkSurface instead of a BackendTexture. Under the hood, however, it seems pretty straight forward (and maybe better because we would avoid re-wrapping the BackendTexture in a new Resource and Proxy).  And for writable shared images that already have an SkSurface, it should be equivalent to call SkSurface::writePixels.\n\n\nIn the short term, that rewrite of the graphite texture backing is probably too big to tackle. Instead I think the begin access calls and CreateSkImage calls that call SkSurfaces::WrapBackendTexture and SkImage::WrapTexture (legacy name is AdoptTextureFrom), should use the texture release proc parameters to be notified of when the DawnTexture is done. Then the ref counting of the created BackendTexture can be managed entirely by WrappedGraphiteTextureBacking and Recorder::deleteBackendTexture() called only when the ref count hits 0 (both backing and release proc are called).",
